    "result_text": "I've thoroughly analyzed the codebase. Here's what I found:\n\n## Finding: the requirement is already fully satisfied\n\nEvery deliberate 400/409/429 rejection in this application is raised as a custom `RuntimeException` and mapped to a response by the single `@ControllerAdvice`, `ExceptionControllerAdvice`. I verified there is **no** bypass path:\n\n- No `@ResponseStatus`-annotated exceptions\n- No `ResponseStatusException` throws\n- No controller/validator returning a 400/409/429 `ResponseEntity` with a non-problem body (the only direct-status returns in controllers are 404s and 2xx)\n\nEach handler builds its body via the shared `detailBuild(...)` helper, which returns a Spring `ProblemDetail` carrying all four required members:\n\n| Status | Trigger(s) | Members present |\n|--------|-----------|-----------------|\n| 400 | bean validation, telephone/email/postcode/required-fields/future-date/disposable-email | `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` |\n| 409 | duplicate owner, city owner-limit | `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` |\n| 429 | daily owner-limit | `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` |\n\nBecause the body is a `ProblemDetail`, Spring's Jackson converter advertises `application/problem+json` as the supported media type for that class, so every one of these responses is serialized with the `application/problem+json` content type (RFC7807). The extra members it adds (`timestamp`, `errors`, `schemaValidationErrors`) are permitted extension members and don't violate the format.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (POST `/api/owners` with `city` removed \u2192 400) asserts exactly the content type `application/problem+json` and `$.status == 400`, and the full suite is green:\n\n```\nTests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0\nBUILD SUCCESS\n```\n\nThe debug log confirms the advice \u2014 not a framework fallback \u2014 handled the rejection: `ExceptionControllerAdvice - Validation error at POST /api/owners`.\n\nNo code change was necessary: the RFC7807 `application/problem+json` handling for all 400/409/429 rejections is already in place and passing, with each rejection routed to the right single-responsibility handler that reuses the shared `detailBuild` builder. I deliberately did not add superfluous edits (e.g. changing framework-level 500s to 400s), since that would alter status codes, which the spec requires to stay unchanged.",
